From openbsd-alpha  Thu May 08 06:40:45 2003
From: Marco Grigull <kni501ss () optushome ! com ! au>
Date: Thu, 08 May 2003 06:40:45 +0000
To: openbsd-alpha
Subject: dhclient
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=openbsd-alpha&m=105237626524738

Hi.
Im just trying out openbsd on the alpha platform for the 1st time.
I noticed that the installer (3.3) did not support dhcp (setting up
from behind an exsisting NAT setup).  So I hardcoded a private 
address that does not clash on my lan.

After the first boot, I run 'dhclient de0' and it picks up a lease
from the i386/3.2 obsd nat box.  However, watching the state table 
pftop on the nat box reveals the alpha box still sourcing requests
(I used lynx as the test) from the old address, and naturally, it
does not connect.

'ifconfig -A' reveals only the new address from the dhcp process,
though 'route -n show' and 'netstat -rn' both reveal both addresses
with the 'H' flag.

The box is an AlphaStstion 200 4/233, with the inbuilt de '21040'

A 'reboot' with 'dhcp NONE NONE NONE' in /etc/hostname.de0 mitigates 
the problem.

Can anyone else confirm this bug?


Cheers
Marco

