On Tue, 20 May 2003, Tim Kornau wrote:

> libio is only available on bwx capable machines
> giving up.

> is this because i have a cpu that does not have the bwx extensions
> enabled ( is this a hardware limitation or software limitation )?

Yes.  It is a hardware limitation, mostly. (*See below)

> AlphaStation 255/233, 232MHz
> cpu0 at mainbus0: ID 0 (primary), 21064A-0 (unknown minor type 0)

This CPU (EV4) does not have the BWX set.  I think you need a EV56
(21164A) or better for BWX support.

You might need to rebuild at least the X chunk of the system from source
so the executable(s) does not contain BWX optimizations.  Or maybe just
rebuild the whole thing if you're patient.  And even so it may not work.

> tga0 at pci0 dev 13 function 0 "DEC TGA2" rev 0x22: TGA2 pass 2,
> board type T8-02
> tga0: 1280 x 1024, 8bpp, Bt485 RAMDAC

Beware that X will be rather slow and ugly on a TGA with this CPU if you
do anything beyond twm or blackbox or something.

I have a Alphastation 4/233 as well, but it runs headless these days.
I can say that Xfree 3.something on Linux, as of about three years ago,
did work without BWX support on the same CPU and card, so the problem
isn't insurmountable.

--
#!/jameel/akari
sleep 4800;
make clean && make breakfast


*As an aside, with Tru64, things built with the Compaq C compiler can
actually emulate extended instructions on older CPUs, if you let the
compiler build that way.  They try to be as backward compatible as
possible so you can install on a brand new GS320 just like an old
AS200 4/233.

